<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karnak</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karnak is a vast temple complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>, dedicated chiefly to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. It was greatly expanded during the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, including a major building programme under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>, and functioned as a religious and economic centre. An Egyptian version of the peace treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> is preserved there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnak.jpg" alt="Karnak" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: René Hourdry · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_de_Louxor_68.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Karnak Temple Complex is a mixture of temples, pylons, chapels and other buildings near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>. The ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> called the area Ipet-isut, the Most Select of Places, and it was the main place of worship of the Theban Triad of the 18th Dynasty, headed by the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. The complex has four main parts: the Precinct of Amun-Re, the largest, the Precinct of Mut, the Precinct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montu">Montu</a> and the dismantled Temple of Amenhotep IV. Its distinctive feature is the length of time over which it was built and used. About thirty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> contributed to it, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> in the Middle Kingdom into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a>, although most surviving buildings date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Major building in the Precinct of Amun-Re took place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> became the capital of the unified country. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a> raised an enclosure wall between the Fourth and Fifth pylons, the earliest part still standing in place. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a> restored the Precinct of Mut and erected twin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisks</a> at the temple entrance; one still stands, the second-tallest ancient obelisk standing anywhere. She also built the Red Chapel, intended as a barque shrine.</p><p>The Great Hypostyle Hall, begun perhaps in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eighteenth-dynasty-of-egypt">Eighteenth Dynasty</a>, was mostly built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>. It covers 5,000 square metres and has 134 columns in 16 rows. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merneptah">Merneptah</a> recorded his victories over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a> on the walls of the Cachette Court. Amenhotep IV, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, built a temple east of the main complex that was destroyed immediately after his death, so thoroughly that its layout is unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i">Nectanebo I</a> of the Thirtieth Dynasty added the First Pylon and the massive enclosure walls. By the 4th century CE Karnak was mostly abandoned, and Christian churches were founded among the ruins.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the exact place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> was unknown. The first European description of the complex was by an unknown Venetian in 1589, and the name Karnak is first attested in 1668, when the Capuchin brothers Protais and Charles François d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> travelled through the area. The first drawing is in Paul Lucas's account of 1704. Scientists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> expedition, among them Vivant Denon, described it in 1798 and 1799. Karnak was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List in 1979 with the rest of Thebes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karnak” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Egyptian Report of Wenamun, from about 1075 BCE, shows King Zakar-Baal receiving an envoy of the temple of Amun at <b>Karnak</b> and demanding payment for cedar, consulting the records of his ancestors and treating Egypt as a trading partner rather than an overlord.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>She sent the celebrated expedition to Punt and sponsored a large building programme at <b>Karnak</b> and Deir el-Bahari.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Kadesh, concluded between Hattusili III and Ramesses II around 1259 BCE, is the earliest peace treaty to survive in the versions of both parties: in Akkadian cuneiform at Hattusa and in Egyptian hieroglyphs at <b>Karnak</b> and the Ramesseum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under its rule during the Twenty-Fifth Dynasty, traditional Egyptian religious and artistic forms were revived, and Taharqa's building works at <b>Karnak</b> and in Nubia rank among the finest of the age.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Senusret I's obelisk still stands at Heliopolis, and his White Chapel at <b>Karnak</b> was rebuilt in modern times from blocks reused in a later pylon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A few years later Shoshenq I campaigned in the region, as recorded in his inscription at <b>Karnak</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
